From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 22:31:15 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] provider: a Codex credit that ran out is not the one that
 runs out first (#5568)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The credits list keeps a card past its expiry until the vendor takes it
off, and magpie read the soonest expiry among those still listed as when
the account's resets run out (Until), and the soonest card as the one
that runs out first (first; Each[0] at the spend, #5555). A card that
ran out already was the soonest then, and it stayed so: the expiring use
of the account waited on that dead card for good ("gone already",
re-armed at every look), so the cards still to run out were never spent
by themselves (#5568, vinwang: two accounts with "before one expires"
on spent nothing and the cards ran out unused), and a spend put the dead
card in the live one's place. first, soonest and each now take now and
skip a credit whose expiry has passed: the soonest is the soonest still
to run out, a spend is of a card that lives, and the card's own list
(Each) says the same. When every one has run out, Until is nil — none
runs out — as when none is held.

Run (darwin/arm64, Go for go1.25):
- RED first: TestCodexResetsRanOutSkipped (until stuck on the dead
  card) and TestUseCodexResetCard ("" spent the dead card) red before,
  green after
- go test ./internal/provider/ -tags nogui: ok
- go build ./...: ok
- Broken by hand: the fix ablated (codex_resets.go back at the parent),
  the two RED tests red again with the report's own shape (until stuck
  on the card past its expiry; the dead card spent in the live one's
  place), and green once restored

Generated-by: GLM-5.3-Flash (ZCode)

The reporter's own reading (a reset spent at 12:24 PT, the card running
out at 14:56, a 30-minute lead set) reads as the #718 design, not as a
bug this fixes: expiringResetSpent spends at once when the account is
held up now (a window at 100%) and won't be free again before the lead's
start — the numbers match a five-hour window stopped until 15:24 (back
>= the lead's start 14:26), which the GUI tooltip and settings' help
disclose (spent at once when held up until after the lead's start). That
half is the maintainer's call; this patch only makes the soonest credit
a live one, which never spends earlier than the lead itself. Not tried
with the reporter's accounts (Windows, PT): the shapes are covered by
the tests, run on darwin/arm64.
